<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  CycloneDDS profile for the MoveIt Pro containers.

  ACTIVE since 2026-08-26: this workspace ran Fast DDS for a while, because the
  prehensio perception servers shipped only rmw_fastrtps_cpp. They ship Cyclone
  now, so the whole graph is back on MoveIt Pro's own default vendor and this is
  the profile that matters. config/fastdds.xml is the fallback for the reverse
  override; see the header of docker-compose.yaml for the full history.

  LOOPBACK, UNICAST, NO MULTICAST, deliberately. It is semantically identical to
  the config MoveIt Pro generates for itself at startup into ~/.ros/cyclonedds.xml
  (lo only, multicast off, single peer 127.0.0.1, MaxAutoParticipantIndex 120),
  and identical to the three sibling copies in robot_ws:
    src/localization/docker/cyclonedds.xml
    src/detection/docker/cyclonedds.xml
    src/isaac/cyclonedds.xml
  Keep the four in sync. A participant that does not adopt this posture is simply
  never discovered by the others, with no error on either side.

  This DOES mean every participant has to be on host networking, since `lo` here
  is the host loopback. MoveIt Pro's containers already are; the robot_ws
  localization and detection stacks were moved onto it for exactly this reason.

  An earlier version of this file used autodetermine + multicast, to reach the
  perception servers while they were still on a docker bridge network. That is
  obsolete now they are host-networked, and actively wrong: multicast on one side
  and off on the other is the mixed posture this comment warns about.

  SocketReceiveBufferSize is raised for the reason MoveIt Pro raises it: image and
  point-cloud traffic overruns the default receive buffer.

  64MB SINCE 2026-10-06, up from 10MB. One /camera0/get_rgbd reply is about 10MB (5MB
  RGBA colour plus 5MB float depth), the size of the old buffer: when the objective
  server was busy for a moment the fragments overran it, the reply arrived late or not
  at all, GetScene timed out after 5 s and took a second picture (an extra projector
  flash). The camera itself served every frame in about 0.4 s, and a separate process
  in the same container received every reply in 0.9 s. Only the receive side is raised.

  max IS THE REQUEST, min THE REFUSAL POINT. Cyclone asks the kernel for max and accepts
  whatever it gets, down to min; below min it refuses to start, and with it every
  container on this profile (MoveIt Pro and the host-networked feature servers). The
  kernel caps the request at net.core.rmem_max, which many machines set to 16 or 32 MB
  following the ROS guides. min therefore stays at the 10MB that ran everywhere before;
  tools/configure_machine.sh raises rmem_max to 64 MiB, so a configured machine gets the
  whole buffer. Checked in the 10.1 runtime image (Cyclone 0.10.5): min 10MB / max 64MB
  starts and sets the buffer, min 64MB alone refuses to start where the kernel cannot
  grant it. On a machine left below 64 MiB, GetScene says so (once, and in every timeout
  message), since Cyclone reports the smaller buffer only in a trace category that is off.
-->
<CycloneDDS
  xmlns="https://cdds.io/config"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="https://cdds.io/config https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eclipse-cyclonedds/cyclonedds/master/etc/cyclonedds.xsd"
>
  <Domain id="any">
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface autodetermine="false" name="lo" />
      </Interfaces>
      <AllowMulticast>false</AllowMulticast>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
      <Peers>
        <Peer Address="127.0.0.1" />
      </Peers>
      <MaxAutoParticipantIndex>120</MaxAutoParticipantIndex>
    </Discovery>
    <Internal>
      <SocketReceiveBufferSize min="10MB" max="64MB" />
    </Internal>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
